# Git Commit & Push — Branch Alignment (Mandatory)

## Default: implement on `main`, branch after success

Git always has a branch checked out — there is no “branchless” state. **Default working branch is `main`.** Do not open or stay on a task branch while implementing.

| Phase | Branch | Action |
|---|---|---|
| Research + implement + verify | `main` | Code and test here until user confirms SUCCESS |
| Commit + push | New task branch | Create branch from `main`, commit, push |
| Follow-up on same slice | Latest related task branch | Reuse if still the same implementation topic |

### When to create a branch

Create a branch **only after** the implementation is verified (user says SUCCESS or equivalent):

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout -b fix/clips-delete-resurrection   # name matches the work
git add <files for this implementation only>
git commit -m "fix: ..."
git push -u origin HEAD
git checkout main
```

### When to reuse a branch (not create a new one)

Stay on the **most recent related** task branch when the new work is a direct continuation of the same implementation (same fix, same feature slice, same refactor slice).

Examples:
- `fix/clips-delete-resurrection` → follow-up tombstone edge case → **same branch**
- `feat/clip-expire-icons` → add expiry preset UI → **same branch**
- `fix/clips-delete-resurrection` → unrelated auth bug → **new** `fix/auth-…` from `main`

### When to leave a branch

After commit/push, return to `main` for the next implementation. Do not leave the checkout on stale `feature/*` branches from prior work.

---

Every `git commit` and `git push` must land on a **task branch whose name matches what is in the commit**. Never commit on an unrelated branch that happened to be checked out.

## Wrong vs right

| Wrong | Why |
|---|---|
| Implement on `feature/extension-safety-expiry-icons` while fixing clip delete | Unrelated branch; work belongs on `main` until SUCCESS, then `fix/clips-delete-resurrection` |
| Commit note-delete fix on `fix/activity-history-html-escape` | Branch topic ≠ diff topic |
| New branch for every tiny follow-up on the same feature | Fragmented history; reuse latest related branch instead |
| Push to `main` without explicit user approval | Breaks stable default branch policy |

| Right | Example |
|---|---|
| Implement on `main`, branch after SUCCESS | Clip delete fix → `fix/clips-delete-resurrection` |
| Follow-up on same slice | More clip-delete hardening → stay on `fix/clips-delete-resurrection` |
| New unrelated implementation | Auth loop fix → new `fix/auth-session-loop` from `main` |

## Before staging (agent checklist)

1. Run `git branch --show-current` and `git status --short`.
2. If still implementing and not yet SUCCESS → should be on **`main`** (not a stale task branch).
3. **Name the work** in 1–2 short phrases from the actual diff.
4. If committing now:
   - **New implementation** → create matching `fix/`, `feat/`, or `refactor/` branch from `main`.
   - **Same implementation follow-up** → use the latest related task branch.
   - **Wrong branch** → stop; do not commit.
5. Stage only files for this implementation.

## Recovery (fix landed on wrong branch)

```bash
git stash push -m "wip"
git checkout main && git pull
git checkout -b fix/correct-topic
git cherry-pick <fix-commit-sha>
git push -u origin fix/correct-topic
git checkout feature/wrong-branch
git reset --hard <commit-before-fix>
git push --force-with-lease origin feature/wrong-branch
git checkout main
```

## Branch naming map (PasteCraft)

| Work in diff | Branch prefix | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Bug / regression | `fix/` | `fix/clips-delete-resurrection` |
| New capability | `feat/` | `feat/clip-expire-icons` |
| Structure move, no behavior change | `refactor/` | `refactor/popup-events-extraction` |
| Docs / rules only | `docs/` | `docs/git-branch-alignment` |

Use **kebab-case**; 2–5 words; no dates in the name.

## Agent must say out loud

Before commit:

> Phase: `<implementing on main | ready to commit>`. Branch: `<current>`. Work: `<summary>`. Action: `<stay on main | create fix/foo | reuse fix/foo>`.

Also run [work-loss-review-formula.mdc](work-loss-review-formula.mdc) / the gate in `git-workflow-commands.mdc` (classify A–E, unmerged SUCCESS, ask Ezequiel: separate vs build-on vs merge-first).

After push, remind: **pushed ≠ merged** — open/merge the PR so the next implementation stacks on `main` instead of racing an unmerged sibling. Testing on `main` after return-from-push will **not** show the feature until merge.

## `git commit all` — multi-scope exception

When the user says **`git commit all`** (see `git-workflow-commands.mdc`):

1. Stash if multiple scopes share one checkout.
2. For **each** scope: `main` → reuse or create matching task branch → stage **only** that scope’s files → commit → push.
3. Return to `main`; drop stash when fully applied.
4. Do **not** mix unrelated scopes in one commit.

Example split (same session):

| Scope | Branch |
|---|---|
| Auth bridge / session restore | `fix/auth-session-bridge` |
| Local test account UI | `feat/local-test-account` |
| Credit pack banner follow-up | `feat/credit-packs-billing` (reuse) |
